The most common modern approach is **Flexbox**:

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  min-height: 100vh;       /* parent needs a height */
}
```

```html
<div class="parent">
  <div class="child">Centered</div>
</div>
```

## Alternatives

**CSS Grid** (shortest):
```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

**Absolute positioning with transform** (useful for overlays or when the parent is `position: relative`):
```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}
.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

**Absolute positioning with `inset` and `margin: auto`** (requires a set width and height on the child):
```css
.child {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 200px;
  height: 100px;
  margin: auto;
}
```

## Notes
- The parent must have a defined height (e.g. `100vh`, or a fixed height), otherwise there's no extra space to center within.
- To center only horizontally, a block element with a set width can use `margin: 0 auto;`.
- For most cases, I'd recommend Grid with `place-items: center` or Flexbox, since both are simple and well supported.

The most common approach is flexbox or grid.

## Flexbox

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  min-height: 100vh;       /* parent needs a height */
}
```

## Grid (shortest)

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

## Absolute positioning

Useful when you want to center relative to a positioned ancestor:

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

## Margin auto in a flex container

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  margin: auto;
}
```

## Notes

- The parent must have a height (e.g. `100vh` or a fixed value), otherwise there is no extra space to center within.
- For centering within the whole page, use `min-height: 100vh` on the parent (or `body`).
- Grid with `place-items: center` is generally the most concise option today. Flexbox is equally well supported and more familiar to many developers.

The simplest modern way is flexbox or grid.

## Option 1: Grid (shortest)

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh; /* parent needs a height */
}
```

```html
<div class="parent">
  <div class="child">Centered</div>
</div>
```

## Option 2: Flexbox

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

## Option 3: Absolute positioning

Useful when the element should be taken out of the normal flow, such as for modals.

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

## Option 4: Margin auto (with a fixed size)

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 200px;
  height: 100px;
  margin: auto;
}
```

## Notes

- The parent must have a height, or there is no extra space to center within. `min-height: 100vh` makes it fill the viewport.
- To center on the whole page, apply the grid or flex rules to `body` (or a full-height wrapper).
- For text alone, `text-align: center` handles horizontal centering. It doesn't affect vertical centering.

I'd recommend Option 1 for most cases, since it needs only three lines and is well supported in all modern browsers.

